From 53e39d8161c6567b85e225d37a9c49e1f1828e86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:33:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Generate a nested function's IL on first invocation, not at
 compile time
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Roadmap item 1-1 (lazy function compilation), taken one layer below where the
item describes it. The item proposes deferring a function's parse and compile,
and names four spec-visible risks: a syntax error inside a never-called function
is still a SyntaxError at compile time; a deferred body must compile against the
scope as it was at closure creation; a direct eval can introduce bindings into
enclosing scopes; generator and async bodies are rewritten into state machines.
Every one of those is a property of the front end, and every one is already
settled by the time a lambda reaches the emitter — the source is parsed, early
errors have thrown, LambdaRewriter has boxed the captures, GeneratorRewriter has
run. What is left is generating machine code for a tree that is already correct.

So only that is deferred. RuntimeMethodBuilder.Relay registers the site instead
of generating it, and MethodRepository.Create — which runs once per closure
instantiation — returns a thunk over that instance's boxes. The first invocation
generates the method, memoized on the shared syntactic site: a function defined
once and instantiated a million times generates once, and one instantiated once
and never called generates never. LambdaRewriter.Rewrite stays eager because it
decides the boxes the creation site references.

Two details are load-bearing and both were found by measuring. Generation runs
on the calling thread with no CompilationStack handoff: matching what the eager
path was inside took the repository suite from 3.5 minutes to over 20, at 12%
CPU, for the reason item 1-2 already recorded about short sources — a fixed
~180us handoff is unaffordable once per function. It is also unnecessary, since
ILCodeGenerator derives from StackGuard and segments only when a tree is
actually deep. And the thunk's warm path is written in IL rather than left to a
call, because a DynamicMethod does not inline what it calls: calling Resolve()
unconditionally cost 1.0247x on a call-heavy script, against 1.0009x for an
inline field read that branches to the slow path only when null.

Compile only, ABBA-interleaved on one build with BROILER_JS_DEFER_IL as the only
difference, one corpus per process, six pairs per arm: jQuery 587 -> 398 ms
(0.661x), Box2D 0.636x, PdfJS 0.689x, Closure 0.714x, Mandreel 0.832x,
allocation 0.52-0.66x throughout. Typescript is 1.034x SLOWER, every pair above
one, and is not explained — its allocation halves like the rest, so it is not
GC. Steady state on a script running 6.3M calls is 1.0009x.

A deferred site retains its expression tree until forced, because sites are
registered under a GCHandle that is never freed; the tree is released the moment
it is generated. That retention is also what made the first measurement of this
change wrong: with six corpora in one process the ones measured last paid for
the ones before them and read 1.6x and 2.6x slower, bimodally. --compile-profile
now takes a corpus name so each is measured in its own process.

Adds DeferredCompilationTests: ten fixtures putting the item's four named risks
against the implementation. Repository suite 7 640 tests, 0 failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
